-- Why: the legal department seats (general counsel, compliance and privacy officer, trust and safety
-- manager, content reviewer) are certified against CIPP/E, CIPM and the ICA International Diploma in
-- Governance, Risk and Compliance. This file authors those three exam banks (40 questions each) at the
-- real exam difficulty: scenario and application items, four options, one key, a two-sentence rationale,
-- the syllabus area named as IAPP and ICA name it, ~25% marked hard, keys spread evenly (10 per letter),
-- and up to 5 items per bank on the UAE variant (Federal Decree-Law 45/2021 PDPL, Consumer Protection
-- Law 15/2020 and the e-commerce rules, Cabinet Decision 10/2019 DNFBP scope).
-- Each bank is guarded by `where not exists (... certification = '<name>')` so the first author wins if
-- two authors share a certification. role_certification rows and the sitter are written elsewhere.
-- Apply: node scripts/apply_sql.mjs scripts/sql/certifications_legal_privacy.sql --apply --ddl

insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select v.cert, v.area, v.diff, v.q, v.a, v.b, v.c, v.d, v.k::char(1), v.r
from (values
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Data Protection Laws','exam',
  'A marketplace established only in Dubai runs a website in French and German, quotes in euro, and ships to buyers in France. It has no EU establishment, servers or staff. Does the GDPR apply to its processing of those buyers personal data?',
  'Yes, Article 3(2)(a) catches a non-EU controller that offers goods or services to data subjects in the Union.',
  'No, the GDPR applies only where the controller has an establishment in the Union.',
  'Only if it stores the data on a server physically located in the Union.',
  'Only if it processes special categories of personal data about those buyers.',
  'A','Language, currency and shipping to the Union together evidence an intention to offer goods to data subjects in the Union, which triggers Article 3(2)(a). Establishment and server location are irrelevant to that limb of the territorial scope test.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Personal Data','exam',
  'A buyer table replaces names with a random buyer_id; the mapping table sits in a separate schema that the analytics team cannot read. How should the analytics dataset be classified?',
  'Anonymous data, because the analytics team cannot re-identify anyone.',
  'Anonymous data, because the identifiers were replaced with random values.',
  'Personal data only for the team that holds the mapping table.',
  'Pseudonymised personal data, and therefore still within the scope of the GDPR.',
  'D','Recital 26 treats data as pseudonymised, not anonymous, while additional information exists anywhere that can attribute it to a person. Scope attaches to the controller as a whole, not to one team''s access rights.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Controllers and Processors','hard',
  'The marketplace and a logistics partner jointly design a delivery-tracking feature, agree together which buyer data it uses and both use the resulting data for their own analytics. What follows under the GDPR?',
  'They are joint controllers and must agree an Article 26 arrangement setting out who does what, the essence of which is made available to data subjects.',
  'The logistics partner is a processor and needs only an Article 28 contract.',
  'Each is an independent controller, so no arrangement between them is required.',
  'The marketplace is the sole controller because it owns the customer relationship.',
  'A','Determining the purposes and means together makes them joint controllers under Article 26, whatever the commercial label. The arrangement must allocate responsibilities, in particular for rights requests and transparency, and its essence must be available to data subjects.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Processing Personal Data','exam',
  'The growth team wants to email named procurement managers at companies that have never traded with the marketplace, using addresses scraped from company websites. What is the correct analysis?',
  'Consent is unnecessary because business contact data is outside the GDPR.',
  'Legitimate interests always covers B2B marketing, so no further steps are needed.',
  'The soft opt-in applies because the recipients are businesses.',
  'A work address that identifies an individual is personal data, so a lawful basis is needed, legitimate interests must be assessed and balanced, and the ePrivacy rules of the recipient state on unsolicited electronic mail still apply.',
  'D','Named work addresses identify individuals and are personal data, so a lawful basis and a legitimate interests assessment are required. Recital 47 permits direct marketing as a legitimate interest but does not displace the national ePrivacy rules on electronic mail.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Data Protection Laws','exam',
  'Under the ePrivacy Directive as implemented across the Union, what is the position on unsolicited marketing email to individual subscribers?',
  'It requires prior consent, subject to a soft opt-in for the sender''s own similar products sold to an existing customer who was offered an opt-out at collection.',
  'It is permitted where the sender has a legitimate interest.',
  'It requires prior consent with no exceptions of any kind.',
  'It is governed solely by Article 6 of the GDPR.',
  'A','Article 13 of the ePrivacy Directive requires prior consent for electronic mail marketing to individual subscribers, with the narrow soft opt-in for existing customers and similar products. Member states differ on corporate subscribers, which is why the recipient state''s implementation must be checked.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Personal Data','exam',
  'Which of these fields held about a supplier contact is NOT personal data?',
  'The address ahmed.k@supplier.ae used by one named employee.',
  'A photograph of the warehouse manager on the supplier profile.',
  'The IP address recorded when that employee logs in.',
  'The company''s trade licence number issued to the legal entity.',
  'D','Data about a legal person, such as its trade licence number, is not personal data unless it identifies an individual. Role-based individual addresses, photographs and IP addresses all relate to identifiable natural persons.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Controllers and Processors','hard',
  'A draft data processing agreement omits one of the Article 28(3) requirements. Which of the following is NOT an Article 28(3) requirement?',
  'The processor processes only on documented instructions from the controller.',
  'The processor assists the controller with data subject rights and with Articles 32 to 36.',
  'At the controller''s choice the processor deletes or returns the personal data at the end of the service.',
  'The processor indemnifies the controller against all regulatory fines.',
  'D','Indemnities are a commercial allocation of risk that the parties may negotiate, but Article 28(3) does not require one. The instruction, assistance and deletion or return clauses are mandatory content of every processing agreement.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Processing Personal Data','exam',
  'What must a legitimate interests assessment establish before the marketplace relies on Article 6(1)(f)?',
  'That the interest is legitimate, that the processing is necessary to achieve it, and that it is not overridden by the interests or fundamental rights of the data subject.',
  'That the data subject has been asked and has not objected.',
  'That the processing is commercially profitable and documented in the record of processing.',
  'That a data protection impact assessment has been completed and filed with the supervisory authority.',
  'A','The three-part test of purpose, necessity and balance is what Article 6(1)(f) requires and what the authority will ask to see. Absence of objection is not the test, and a DPIA is required only where Article 35 is triggered.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Information Provision Obligations','exam',
  'The marketplace buys a list of supplier contacts from a trade body. By when must it give those contacts the Article 14 information?',
  'Immediately on receipt of the list, in every case.',
  'Only if and when it first contacts them, with no outer limit.',
  'Within a reasonable period and at the latest one month, or at first communication if that is earlier.',
  'Within seventy-two hours of obtaining the data.',
  'C','Article 14(3) sets a reasonable period and an outer limit of one month, brought forward to the first communication with the data subject. Seventy-two hours is the breach notification deadline, not a transparency deadline.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Data Subject Rights','exam',
  'A buyer submits a subject access request covering four years of RFQ history. Which response is correct?',
  'Provide a copy free of charge within one month, extendable by two further months for complexity, with the extension notified within the first month.',
  'Charge a standard administrative fee for any request covering more than one year.',
  'Refuse the request because RFQ history is commercially confidential.',
  'Respond within forty days as required by Article 15.',
  'A','Article 12 sets one month, extendable by two months for complexity or number of requests provided the data subject is told within the first month. A fee may be charged only for manifestly unfounded or excessive requests, or for further copies.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Data Subject Rights','hard',
  'A buyer asks for erasure of everything held about him, including six invoices issued this year. What should the marketplace do?',
  'Erase everything, since the right to erasure is absolute once the account is closed.',
  'Refuse the request in full because one exemption applies to part of it.',
  'Erase the marketing profile but keep the invoices, tell him which data is retained and why, and rely on the legal obligation and legal claims grounds in Article 17(3).',
  'Suspend the account and decide when the statutory retention period expires.',
  'C','Article 17(3) disapplies erasure where processing is necessary for compliance with a legal obligation or for legal claims, which covers statutory accounting records. The exemption is applied field by field and the outcome, with reasons, must be explained to the data subject.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Data Subject Rights','exam',
  'A supplier contact objects to receiving marketing emails but the marketplace believes its legitimate interest is compelling. What is the correct outcome?',
  'The objection is weighed against the marketplace''s compelling interest.',
  'The objection applies only after a thirty-day cooling-off period.',
  'Processing for direct marketing must stop immediately and for good; no balancing is available.',
  'The objection is valid only if made in writing on the form provided.',
  'C','Article 21(2) and 21(3) make objection to direct marketing absolute, with no balancing test. The controller must stop that processing, although it may keep a suppression record to honour the objection.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Security of Processing','exam',
  'How does Article 32 define the security measures a controller must implement?',
  'The measures listed in ISO 27001 Annex A, in full.',
  'Whatever the processor already provides as standard.',
  'Encryption of all personal data at rest and in transit, without exception.',
  'Measures appropriate to the risk, having regard to the state of the art, cost and the nature and severity of the risk, including as appropriate pseudonymisation and encryption and the ability to restore availability after an incident.',
  'D','Article 32 is risk-based and outcome-based rather than a fixed control list; encryption and pseudonymisation are named as appropriate measures, not universal mandates. Restoration of availability and regular testing of the measures are part of the same obligation.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Security of Processing','hard',
  'An export of quotation records including buyer names and phone numbers is emailed to the wrong supplier, who confirms deletion. What is the obligation?',
  'No action, because the recipient confirmed deletion.',
  'Record the breach internally, and notify the supervisory authority within seventy-two hours of becoming aware unless the breach is unlikely to result in a risk to rights and freedoms; notify the data subjects only if the risk is high.',
  'Notify the supervisory authority and every data subject within seventy-two hours in all cases.',
  'Notify the data subjects within one month under Article 14.',
  'B','Article 33 sets the seventy-two hour authority deadline with an unlikely-to-result-in-a-risk exemption, and Article 34 sets a separate high-risk threshold for telling individuals. Article 33(5) requires every breach to be documented whether or not it is notified.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Cross-Border Data Transfers','exam',
  'The marketplace moves EU buyer data to its own Dubai systems. There is no adequacy decision for the UAE. What is the correct route?',
  'Rely on the buyer''s acceptance of the terms and conditions as explicit consent.',
  'Use the 2021 standard contractual clauses, carry out and document a transfer impact assessment on the destination country''s law and practice, and add supplementary measures where the assessment shows they are needed.',
  'Rely on Article 49 because the transfers are part of the ordinary business.',
  'No safeguard is needed because the marketplace is the controller at both ends.',
  'B','Schrems II requires the exporter to assess the destination law and practice alongside the clauses, not merely sign them. Article 49 derogations are for occasional, non-repetitive transfers and cannot carry a routine data flow.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Cross-Border Data Transfers','exam',
  'What is the practical effect of an adequacy decision for a third country?',
  'It removes the GDPR from the transferred data entirely.',
  'It obliges the importer to appoint an EU representative.',
  'Personal data may flow to that country without any further specific authorisation or transfer tool, although the rest of the GDPR still applies to the exporter.',
  'It permits transfers only to companies certified under a national scheme.',
  'C','Article 45 lets data flow to an adequate country as if it were an intra-Union transfer, with no additional transfer tool needed. Every other GDPR obligation on the exporter, including lawful basis and security, continues to apply.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Supervision and Enforcement','exam',
  'The Dubai marketplace has no establishment in the Union but targets buyers in France, Germany and Spain. Which statement is correct?',
  'It has no main establishment, so the one-stop-shop does not apply; it must appoint an Article 27 representative in one of those member states and may be addressed by each supervisory authority concerned.',
  'It may nominate the French authority as its lead supervisory authority by written notice.',
  'The European Data Protection Board acts as its single point of contact.',
  'Only the authority of the member state with the most buyers has competence.',
  'A','The one-stop-shop under Article 56 depends on a main establishment in the Union, which a purely third-country controller lacks. It must instead appoint a representative under Article 27 and answer to each competent authority.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Supervision and Enforcement','hard',
  'An authority finds that the marketplace processed buyer data without any lawful basis and also failed to keep an Article 30 record. Which fine ceilings apply?',
  'Both breaches fall in the lower tier of 10 million euro or 2 per cent of worldwide annual turnover.',
  'Both breaches fall in the higher tier of 20 million euro or 4 per cent.',
  'The ceilings are cumulative and are added together.',
  'The lawful basis breach falls in the higher tier of 20 million euro or 4 per cent of total worldwide annual turnover, whichever is higher; the records failure falls in the lower tier of 10 million euro or 2 per cent.',
  'D','Article 83(5) reserves the higher tier for the basic principles, lawful basis, data subject rights and transfers; Article 83(4) covers controller and processor obligations such as Articles 25, 28, 30, 32 and 33. Article 83(3) caps a single set of linked operations at the highest applicable amount rather than adding the ceilings.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Compliance','exam',
  'When must the marketplace designate a data protection officer?',
  'Whenever it employs more than 250 people.',
  'Where its core activities require regular and systematic monitoring of data subjects on a large scale, or large-scale processing of special categories or criminal conviction data, or where it is a public authority.',
  'Whenever it transfers personal data outside the Union.',
  'Only when the supervisory authority orders it after an inspection.',
  'B','Article 37(1) sets three triggers and headcount is not one of them. Member state law may add further mandatory cases, and a voluntary DPO is then held to the same Articles 38 and 39 standards.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Compliance','exam',
  'The marketplace plans automated scoring of every supplier using behavioural and financial data to decide who may quote. Is a DPIA required?',
  'No, because the scoring concerns businesses.',
  'No, unless the supervisory authority asks for one.',
  'Yes, systematic and extensive evaluation based on automated processing, including profiling, on which decisions producing legal or similarly significant effects are based, is a listed Article 35(3) trigger.',
  'Yes, but only after the system has gone live and produced its first decisions.',
  'C','Article 35(3)(a) names exactly this kind of automated evaluation, and the decision to exclude a supplier from quoting is a similarly significant effect on the individuals behind that supplier. A DPIA must be carried out before the processing starts.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Processing Personal Data','exam',
  'Data collected to fulfil RFQs is now to be reused to train a demand forecasting model. What must be considered first?',
  'Nothing, since the data is already lawfully held.',
  'Only whether the model output is anonymous.',
  'Whether the new purpose is compatible with the original one under Article 6(4), considering the link between purposes, the context, the nature of the data, the consequences and the safeguards; if not compatible, a fresh lawful basis and new information are needed.',
  'Whether the data subjects have complained about the reuse.',
  'C','Purpose limitation in Article 5(1)(b) allows further processing only where it is compatible, and Article 6(4) sets the factors for that test. Where the test fails, the controller needs a separate lawful basis and must inform the data subjects of the new purpose.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Data Subject Rights','hard',
  'A buyer demands that his order history be sent in machine-readable form to a competing platform. The marketplace processes that history on the legitimate interests basis. What is the position?',
  'Portability applies to all personal data regardless of the lawful basis.',
  'Portability under Article 20 applies only to data the data subject provided, processed by automated means, on the basis of consent or of a contract; legitimate interests data falls outside it, although the right of access still applies.',
  'Portability applies but the marketplace may charge a transmission fee.',
  'Portability applies only where the receiving controller is established in the Union.',
  'B','Article 20(1) limits portability to consent-based or contract-based automated processing of data provided by the data subject. Data held on legitimate interests is reachable by an access request but not by a portability request.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Information Provision Obligations','exam',
  'The sign-up page uses a short notice with a link to the full privacy statement. Is that compliant?',
  'No, all Article 13 information must appear on the collection screen itself.',
  'Yes, a layered notice is acceptable provided the first layer is concise, intelligible and in clear plain language and the full Article 13 information is easily accessible at the point of collection.',
  'Yes, provided the user ticks a box confirming they have read it.',
  'No, unless the notice is also sent by email.',
  'B','Article 12(1) requires concise, transparent, intelligible and easily accessible information, which the EDPB guidance implements as layering. The critical points, such as purposes, lawful basis, recipients, transfers and rights, must be reachable at the moment of collection.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Controllers and Processors','exam',
  'A cloud processor wants to move part of the service to a new sub-processor. What does Article 28 require?',
  'Prior specific written authorisation from the controller, or general written authorisation with prior notice of intended additions or replacements so the controller can object, and the same data protection obligations flowed down by contract.',
  'Nothing, since the processor remains liable in any case.',
  'Approval by the supervisory authority before the change.',
  'A new controller-to-controller agreement with the sub-processor.',
  'A','Article 28(2) requires specific or general prior written authorisation with a right to object, and 28(4) requires the same obligations to be imposed on the sub-processor. The original processor remains fully liable to the controller for the sub-processor''s performance.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Security of Processing','exam',
  'The marketplace''s email provider, acting as processor, discovers that a misconfiguration exposed buyer messages. What must the provider do?',
  'Notify the supervisory authority within seventy-two hours in its own name.',
  'Notify the controller without undue delay; the controller then assesses the risk and makes any Article 33 and Article 34 notifications.',
  'Notify the affected data subjects directly to save time.',
  'Wait until the internal investigation is complete before telling anyone.',
  'B','Article 33(2) places one duty on the processor: tell the controller without undue delay. Assessment of risk and notification to the authority and to individuals are the controller''s decisions.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Cross-Border Data Transfers','hard',
  'One EU buyer needs an urgent shipment and asks the marketplace to send his contact details to a courier in a third country with no adequacy decision and no clauses in place. Can Article 49 be used?',
  'Yes, the transfer is necessary for the performance of a contract with the data subject and is occasional, which is an Article 49(1)(b) derogation; it cannot be turned into a routine channel for all shipments.',
  'Yes, and the same derogation may then cover every future shipment to that courier.',
  'No, Article 49 applies only to public interest transfers.',
  'No, derogations were removed by the Schrems II judgment.',
  'A','Article 49(1)(b) permits a transfer necessary for the performance of a contract with the data subject, but the EDPB reads the derogations as occasional and non-repetitive. A recurring flow must be put on standard contractual clauses or another Article 46 tool.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Compliance','exam',
  'The marketplace employs 90 people. Must it keep an Article 30 record of processing activities?',
  'No, the exemption for organisations under 250 employees is unconditional.',
  'No, records are required only of processors.',
  'Yes, the under-250 exemption falls away where the processing is likely to result in a risk to rights and freedoms, is not occasional, or involves special categories or criminal conviction data, and routine customer processing is not occasional.',
  'Yes, but only for processing carried out outside the Union.',
  'C','Article 30(5) makes the small-organisation exemption conditional, and ongoing customer and supplier processing is by definition not occasional. In practice almost every trading business must maintain the record.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Data Protection Laws','exam',
  'How do Articles 7 and 8 of the Charter of Fundamental Rights relate to data protection?',
  'Article 8 is the only relevant provision; privacy of communications is not engaged.',
  'Article 7 protects private and family life, home and communications, while Article 8 sets out a separate right to the protection of personal data with fair processing, a legitimate basis, access, rectification and independent supervision.',
  'They are two labels for a single right with identical content.',
  'They apply only to the institutions of the Union and never to companies.',
  'B','The Charter treats privacy and data protection as distinct but connected rights, and Article 8 is the constitutional root of the GDPR''s principles and of independent supervisory authorities. The Court routinely reads GDPR provisions in light of both articles.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Personal Data','hard',
  'Which of these datasets is a special category of personal data under Article 9?',
  'A supplier''s bank account number used to pay invoices.',
  'A photograph of an employee stored in the HR file for identification by a human.',
  'The nationality recorded on a delivery driver''s licence check.',
  'A record that a warehouse worker is a member of a trade union.',
  'D','Article 9(1) lists trade union membership expressly, while bank details, nationality and ordinary photographs are not special categories. A photograph becomes biometric special category data only when processed through specific technical means for unique identification.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Supervision and Enforcement','exam',
  'Where must the Article 27 representative of a non-EU controller be established?',
  'In the member state where the controller''s main bank is located.',
  'In any member state chosen by the controller, with no connection to the data subjects.',
  'In one of the member states where the data subjects whose data is processed in connection with the offering of goods or services are located.',
  'In Belgium, so that the European Data Protection Board can reach it.',
  'C','Article 27(3) ties the representative''s location to the member states where the affected data subjects are. The representative is the addressee for data subjects and authorities on all issues relating to the processing.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Processing Personal Data','exam',
  'Consent to marketing is collected by a pre-ticked box in the middle of the terms and conditions acceptance. What is wrong with it?',
  'Nothing, because the user could have unticked it.',
  'Consent is not freely given, specific, informed or an unambiguous indication by a clear affirmative action, and bundling it with the acceptance of terms breaches Articles 4(11) and 7(4).',
  'It is valid but must be refreshed every twelve months.',
  'It is valid only if the user is over eighteen.',
  'B','Recital 32 rules out pre-ticked boxes and silence as consent, and Article 7(4) treats bundling with contract acceptance as evidence that consent is not free. Consent must also be as easy to withdraw as to give.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Information Provision Obligations','exam',
  'A privacy notice says only that data may be transferred abroad. What further transparency does the GDPR require?',
  'Nothing, a general statement suffices.',
  'A list of every individual recipient by name and address.',
  'The technical location of every server used.',
  'The fact of the transfer, the third country, whether an adequacy decision exists or which Article 46 safeguard is used, and the means to obtain a copy of that safeguard or where it has been made available.',
  'D','Articles 13(1)(f) and 14(1)(f) require the country, the legal basis of the transfer and reference to the appropriate safeguards, including how to obtain a copy. Naming every recipient is not required, though categories of recipients are.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Compliance','hard',
  'Before appointing a new document-processing vendor, what does the GDPR require of the marketplace as controller?',
  'To use only processors that provide sufficient guarantees of appropriate technical and organisational measures, to verify those guarantees through due diligence, and to record the assessment; an Article 28 contract alone is not enough.',
  'To obtain the supervisory authority''s prior approval of the vendor.',
  'To require the vendor to hold ISO 27001 certification as a matter of law.',
  'To transfer liability to the vendor by contract.',
  'A','Article 28(1) is a substantive obligation to select competent processors, not merely to paper the relationship. Certifications and codes of conduct are evidence of guarantees under Article 28(5) but are not themselves mandatory, and liability cannot be contracted away from the controller.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Data Subject Rights','exam',
  'The marketplace automatically rejects supplier applications below a machine-generated risk score, with no human involvement. What does Article 22 require?',
  'Nothing, because the decision concerns a business application.',
  'A lawful basis under Article 22(2) such as necessity for a contract, authorisation by law or explicit consent, plus safeguards including meaningful information about the logic and the right to obtain human intervention, to express a point of view and to contest the decision.',
  'Only that the score be disclosed on request.',
  'That every applicant consent in writing before applying.',
  'B','Article 22(1) prohibits solely automated decisions with legal or similarly significant effects unless an Article 22(2) exception applies, and Article 22(3) requires the safeguards. Articles 13(2)(f) and 15(1)(h) add the transparency duty about the logic involved.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Security of Processing','exam',
  'A closed buyer account still holds full RFQ and message history four years later, with no retention rule. Which principle is breached?',
  'Accuracy, under Article 5(1)(d).',
  'Purpose limitation, under Article 5(1)(b).',
  'Lawfulness, under Article 6.',
  'Storage limitation, under Article 5(1)(e), which requires personal data to be kept in identifiable form no longer than necessary for the purposes.',
  'D','Article 5(1)(e) requires defined retention periods and deletion or anonymisation once the purpose is exhausted, subject to statutory retention. Article 13(2)(a) also requires the retention period, or the criteria for it, to be disclosed.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Data Protection Laws','exam',
  'How does the lawful basis structure of UAE Federal Decree-Law 45/2021 on personal data protection differ from the GDPR?',
  'It is identical to Article 6 of the GDPR.',
  'It recognises only consent, with no exceptions at all.',
  'The PDPL makes the data subject''s consent the default requirement and then lists the cases in which processing may proceed without consent, whereas the GDPR sets out six equal lawful bases of which consent is only one.',
  'It has no concept of a lawful basis.',
  'C','Article 4 of the PDPL prohibits processing without consent and then enumerates exceptions such as contract performance, legal obligation, protection of the public interest and legal claims. The practical drafting effect is that UAE notices are built around consent plus exceptions rather than a free choice among six bases.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Cross-Border Data Transfers','exam',
  'Under the UAE PDPL, when may personal data be transferred outside the UAE?',
  'Never, all personal data must remain in the UAE.',
  'Where the destination has an adequate level of protection recognised by the UAE Data Office or is bound by an applicable international agreement, or, failing that, on a contractual undertaking, express consent of the data subject, or another listed exception such as necessity for a contract or for legal claims.',
  'Only where the destination has an EU adequacy decision.',
  'Only where the data subject is a UAE national.',
  'B','Articles 22 and 23 of the PDPL build an adequacy route plus contract, consent and necessity exceptions, structurally similar to but not identical with the GDPR. Free-zone regimes such as the DIFC and ADGM apply their own transfer rules instead.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Security of Processing','hard',
  'A UAE-only breach affects UAE buyers on a system governed by the PDPL. How does the notification duty differ from the GDPR?',
  'The PDPL also sets a seventy-two hour deadline to the regulator.',
  'The PDPL requires no notification of any kind.',
  'The PDPL requires the controller to notify the UAE Data Office immediately upon becoming aware of a breach that would prejudice the privacy, confidentiality or security of the data, and to notify the data subject where the breach would cause harm, without stating a fixed hour count; the GDPR fixes seventy-two hours to the authority.',
  'The PDPL requires notification only after the investigation closes.',
  'C','Article 9 of the PDPL uses an immediacy standard and a harm threshold for telling individuals rather than the GDPR''s numeric deadline and high-risk threshold. A controller caught by both must run to the stricter seventy-two hour clock in practice.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Compliance','exam',
  'A UAE consumer buys through the marketplace and complains that the price shown excluded fees. Which UAE requirement is most directly engaged?',
  'Federal Decree-Law 45/2021 on personal data protection.',
  'Cabinet Decision 10/2019 on anti-money laundering.',
  'Federal Decree-Law 14/2023 on trade by modern technological means, which governs licensing of the platform only.',
  'Consumer Protection Law 15/2020 and its implementing regulation, which require the supplier to display the price clearly and completely, disclose the terms of sale, and honour the consumer''s rights of return and repair.',
  'D','Price transparency and disclosure of contract terms sit in the consumer protection regime, not the data or AML regimes. The electronic transactions and e-commerce rules add supplier identity and record obligations on top of the same disclosure duties.'),
 ('CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)','Compliance','exam',
  'Is a general B2B goods marketplace a designated non-financial business or profession under Cabinet Decision 10/2019?',
  'Not as such; the DNFBP categories are brokers and agents in real estate, dealers in precious metals and precious stones above the cash threshold, auditors, and company or trust service providers, and a business falls in scope only if it carries on one of those activities.',
  'Yes, every online marketplace is a DNFBP.',
  'Yes, because it processes payments for third parties.',
  'No, DNFBP status attaches only to companies licensed in a financial free zone.',
  'A','Cabinet Decision 10/2019 lists the DNFBP categories exhaustively, and platform status alone does not create one. If the platform itself brokers real estate or trades precious metals and stones above the cash threshold, the registration, reporting and record duties, including goAML, are triggered.'),

 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Developing a Privacy Programme','exam',
  'The board asks why the marketplace should fund a privacy programme. Which business case is strongest by CIPM standards?',
  'Privacy is a legal requirement, so no further justification is needed.',
  'Competitors have a programme, so the marketplace should too.',
  'A privacy incident would be embarrassing for the founder.',
  'A case tied to the organisation''s own risk appetite and objectives: quantified regulatory and contractual exposure, cost of a breach, revenue at risk from enterprise buyers who require privacy assurances, and the efficiency gained from a single data inventory.',
  'D','CIPM expects the business case to be framed in the organisation''s own risk and revenue language, not as an abstract legal duty. Tying privacy to deal enablement and to measurable exposure is what moves budget.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Programme Framework','hard',
  'The marketplace operates in the UAE and sells into the EU and Saudi Arabia. Which framework approach fits best?',
  'Apply UAE law only, and handle other jurisdictions on complaint.',
  'Set a single global baseline at the highest common standard and layer jurisdiction-specific requirements on top, documenting the delta per country.',
  'Maintain fully separate programmes with no shared controls.',
  'Adopt the GDPR verbatim as the global standard and ignore local law.',
  'B','A global baseline with local deltas gives one operating model while still meeting each regime, and it is the approach CIPM teaches for multinational programmes. Adopting one law verbatim fails wherever local law is stricter or differently structured, such as PDPL consent or Saudi localisation.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Programme: Establishing Programme Governance','exam',
  'Country teams complain that the central privacy office is too slow to approve local marketing. Which governance model addresses this?',
  'Hybrid, with the privacy office owning policy, standards and escalation while trained local privacy champions execute and decide within defined thresholds.',
  'Local, with each country setting its own rules independently.',
  'Centralised, with all decisions retained by the privacy office.',
  'None; approval speed is a resourcing issue and not a governance one.',
  'A','The hybrid model keeps a single policy voice while pushing routine decisions to embedded champions, which is the standard CIPM answer to a central bottleneck. Fully local governance loses consistency and fully central governance recreates the delay.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Assess','exam',
  'Where should a privacy programme begin its operational lifecycle?',
  'With employee training, so awareness comes first.',
  'With a breach response plan, because incidents are the biggest risk.',
  'With drafting the external privacy notice.',
  'With a data inventory and mapping exercise establishing what personal data is held, why, where it flows, who it is shared with and how long it is kept.',
  'D','Every other activity, from notices to retention to breach scoping, depends on knowing the data. CIPM places assess, and specifically the inventory and data flow map, at the front of the operational lifecycle.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Protect','exam',
  'Engineering proposes to add supplier credit scoring to the platform. When should privacy be engaged?',
  'At user acceptance testing, when the data flows are concrete.',
  'At requirements and design, so that privacy by design and by default is built into the specification, with a threshold assessment deciding whether a full impact assessment is needed.',
  'After launch, during the first privacy audit.',
  'Only if the legal team receives a complaint.',
  'B','Privacy by design requires intervention while requirements are still cheap to change, and the threshold assessment is the standard gate. Engaging at UAT or later converts design defects into expensive retrofits.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Programme Framework','hard',
  'Which metric best demonstrates to the audit committee that the privacy programme is improving?',
  'A trend line combining leading and lagging indicators: percentage of new systems passing a privacy threshold assessment before launch, mean time to close a data subject request, percentage of high-risk vendors with completed due diligence, and repeat findings closed.',
  'The number of training slides produced.',
  'The number of privacy policies published this year.',
  'The absence of regulatory fines to date.',
  'A','CIPM expects a balanced set of leading and lagging measures trended over time, not activity counts. The absence of a fine measures luck and regulator attention rather than programme effectiveness.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Sustain','exam',
  'What is the difference between privacy monitoring and a privacy audit?',
  'Monitoring is continuous and performed by the process owners and the privacy function to detect drift, while an audit is a periodic, independent and evidence-based assessment against a defined standard.',
  'They are the same activity under two names.',
  'Monitoring is performed by the external regulator and audits by management.',
  'Audits are continuous and monitoring is annual.',
  'A','CIPM distinguishes the ongoing first- and second-line monitoring from independent third-line assurance. Independence and a defined criteria set are what make an audit an audit.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Respond','exam',
  'A data subject request arrives through a support chat rather than the privacy inbox. What should the programme have in place?',
  'A rule that only requests to the privacy inbox are valid.',
  'A rule that support agents forward the request to legal for a decision on validity.',
  'Nothing; the requester should be told to resubmit.',
  'Intake procedures covering every channel, agent training to recognise and escalate a request, a logged clock starting from receipt anywhere in the organisation, and an identity verification step.',
  'D','Requests are valid however they arrive, so the clock starts on organisational receipt and intake must be channel-agnostic. Training front-line staff to recognise requests is a core CIPM operational control.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Programme: Establishing Programme Governance','hard',
  'The privacy officer reports to the general counsel, who also negotiates the contracts that the privacy officer must review. What is the concern and the fix?',
  'The concern is a conflict of interest that can compromise independent privacy advice; the fix is a documented escalation route to the board or audit committee, and a conflict register with recusal for the affected reviews.',
  'The concern is cost duplication; the fix is to merge the roles.',
  'There is no concern; legal reporting lines are the norm.',
  'The concern is confidentiality; the fix is to stop reviewing contracts.',
  'A','Legal reporting lines are common but the reviewer must not be captured by the deal owner, and the GDPR expresses the same principle for DPO independence. An independent escalation path plus recusal preserves the advice without restructuring the department.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Assess','exam',
  'A new supplier onboarding vendor will process buyer and supplier contact data. What is the correct assessment sequence?',
  'Risk-tier the vendor, run due diligence proportionate to the tier, execute the data processing terms with security and sub-processor clauses, and only then release production data, with re-assessment on a schedule tied to the tier.',
  'Execute the contract, then run due diligence during the first year of service.',
  'Rely on the vendor''s public certifications and skip due diligence.',
  'Ask the vendor to complete a questionnaire after go-live.',
  'A','Third-party risk management assesses before data moves, and the depth of the assessment follows the risk tier. Certifications are evidence within due diligence, not a substitute for it, and re-assessment keeps the picture current.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Protect','exam',
  'What does data minimisation mean operationally for a quotation form?',
  'Collect everything available and delete later.',
  'Collect only the fields necessary for the stated purpose, challenge each field against its purpose at design time, and remove fields no downstream process consumes.',
  'Collect additional fields but mark them optional.',
  'Collect fields freely provided they are encrypted.',
  'B','Minimisation is a design-time discipline of justifying every field against a purpose, not a storage or encryption question. Optional fields still create records that must be secured, disclosed and deleted.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Sustain','exam',
  'Awareness training has been delivered once at induction. What does a sustaining programme add?',
  'Role-based recurring training, targeted refreshers after incidents or process changes, measurable comprehension rather than attendance, and reinforcement through the channels staff already use.',
  'An annual certificate of attendance for the file.',
  'Nothing further is required once induction training is complete.',
  'A single all-staff email each year.',
  'A','CIPM treats awareness as a continuous, role-tailored programme measured by comprehension and behaviour change. Attendance records prove delivery, not effectiveness.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Respond','hard',
  'During a suspected breach, business owners argue about whether it is notifiable while the clock runs. What should the response plan already contain?',
  'Pre-agreed severity criteria and a decision owner, a documented assessment template, named legal and communications leads, notification templates, regulator contact details per jurisdiction, and evidence-preservation instructions.',
  'A requirement that the chief executive personally approve every step.',
  'A rule that all incidents are notified to be safe.',
  'A rule that no incident is notified without external counsel sign-off.',
  'A','A response plan works by removing decisions from the critical path in advance, which is exactly what pre-agreed criteria and named owners do. Blanket notification and blanket external sign-off both degrade the response instead of steering it.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Programme Framework','exam',
  'What is the purpose of assessing the programme against a maturity model?',
  'To satisfy the external auditor that policies exist.',
  'To establish a repeatable baseline, target a defined level per capability, and show trend over time so that investment is directed at the weakest capability rather than the loudest one.',
  'To benchmark headcount against competitors.',
  'To replace the need for control testing.',
  'B','A maturity assessment turns a scattered control picture into a prioritised roadmap with a measurable baseline and target. It complements control testing rather than replacing it.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Assess','exam',
  'Marketing has run a campaign on a new channel without telling the privacy office. What structural control prevents recurrence?',
  'Discipline for the marketing lead.',
  'A gate in the existing process the team must already pass, such as a privacy threshold assessment attached to campaign approval or budget release, backed by a privacy champion inside marketing.',
  'A quarterly reminder email to marketing.',
  'A new stand-alone privacy policy document.',
  'B','Controls work when they sit inside the workflow people already follow rather than beside it. Embedding a champion and a gate converts privacy from an optional consultation into a step of the process.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Protect','exam',
  'What does privacy by default require of a new marketplace feature that offers profile visibility settings?',
  'That the most permissive setting be preselected so the feature is discoverable.',
  'That no default be set and the user be forced to choose.',
  'That defaults be reviewed annually.',
  'That, without any intervention by the individual, only personal data necessary for each specific purpose is processed, so the least permissive setting is the default and broader sharing is an active opt-in.',
  'D','Article 25(2) states the default rule in terms of amount, extent, storage period and accessibility, and CIPM implements it as least-permissive defaults. Discoverability is a design problem to be solved without weakening the default.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Sustain','hard',
  'A control that was effective at the last audit has quietly stopped operating because the underlying system was replaced. What sustaining mechanism should have caught this?',
  'The annual policy review.',
  'The induction training programme.',
  'The external privacy notice review.',
  'Change management integration, so that system and process changes trigger a privacy review and a refresh of the data inventory and control mapping, coupled with periodic control operating-effectiveness testing.',
  'D','Controls decay when the environment changes underneath them, which is why CIPM ties the programme to change management and to recurring operating-effectiveness testing. Reviewing policies or notices does not test whether a control still runs.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Respond','exam',
  'A buyer asks to see everything held about him. The data sits in six systems including a support tool and a data warehouse. What does the programme need?',
  'A rule that only the primary account system is searched.',
  'A documented request-fulfilment procedure mapped to the data inventory, naming the systems in scope, the owner for each, the extraction method, the redaction rules for third-party data, and the review step before release.',
  'A copy of the database exported to the requester.',
  'External counsel to run each request.',
  'B','Fulfilment is an operational process built on the inventory, with per-system owners and a redaction and review step before anything leaves. Raw exports risk disclosing other people''s personal data and internal privileged material.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Programme: Establishing Programme Governance','exam',
  'Which stakeholder group is most often missed when a privacy programme is established, and why does it matter?',
  'The board, because it sets risk appetite.',
  'Procurement and vendor management, because contracts and vendor selection determine where personal data goes and what controls travel with it.',
  'The external auditor, because it signs the accounts.',
  'The marketing team, because it drafts the privacy notice.',
  'B','Procurement decides which processors are engaged and on what terms, so a programme that misses it loses control of third-party data flows. CIPM treats procurement as a primary partner of the privacy function.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Developing a Privacy Programme','exam',
  'What should a privacy programme charter contain?',
  'Only the names of the privacy team members.',
  'Only the applicable legal citations.',
  'Scope, objectives aligned to business goals, governance model and decision rights, roles and responsibilities, the escalation route, the reporting cadence and the metrics by which the programme is judged.',
  'The full text of the data processing agreements in use.',
  'C','The charter is the mandate: what the programme covers, who decides what, and how success is measured and reported. Legal citations and contract text belong in the standards and templates the charter authorises.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Assess','hard',
  'A DPIA on a supplier scoring feature concludes that a high residual risk remains after mitigation. What must happen next?',
  'Proceed and record the risk in the register.',
  'Cancel the feature automatically.',
  'Seek the supervisory authority''s prior consultation under Article 36 before starting the processing, presenting the DPIA, the measures considered and the residual risk.',
  'Obtain consent from every affected individual instead.',
  'C','Article 36 makes prior consultation mandatory where a DPIA shows high residual risk that the controller cannot mitigate. Recording the risk or substituting consent does not discharge that duty.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Protect','exam',
  'Which retention approach is defensible for RFQ records?',
  'Keep everything indefinitely so nothing is lost.',
  'Delete everything after ninety days regardless of context.',
  'Let each team decide for its own systems.',
  'A retention schedule per record class, each period justified by purpose or statute, implemented as an automated rule in the source system with documented legal holds and evidence of execution.',
  'D','A defensible schedule ties each period to a purpose or a statutory requirement and can prove that deletion actually happened. Indefinite retention, arbitrary uniform periods and team-by-team discretion all fail on evidence.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Sustain','exam',
  'The privacy office wants a monthly report the executive team will actually read. What should it contain?',
  'A short set of trended metrics against target, the top risks with owners and due dates, incidents and their status, and decisions the executive team must make.',
  'The full data inventory.',
  'A list of every data subject request received.',
  'The complete text of the latest regulatory guidance.',
  'A','Executive reporting is exception- and decision-focused, trended against targets, with clear ownership. Raw inventories and request logs belong in the operational record, not the executive pack.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Respond','exam',
  'After a confirmed breach the programme completes notification. What closes the incident properly?',
  'Closing the ticket once the regulator acknowledges receipt.',
  'A root cause analysis, corrective actions with owners and dates, an update to the risk register and the data inventory where the incident revealed inaccuracies, a lessons-learned review, and verification that the corrective actions were implemented.',
  'A press statement and an internal announcement.',
  'A payment to the affected individuals.',
  'B','The respond phase ends by feeding the lifecycle: root cause, corrective action, verification and updated documentation. Regulator acknowledgement closes the notification, not the incident.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Programme Framework','hard',
  'The marketplace signs an enterprise buyer whose contract demands audit rights, deletion certificates and forty-eight hour breach notice to the buyer. How should the programme respond?',
  'Refuse the clauses as non-standard.',
  'Sign and manage the obligations by exception when they arise.',
  'Track the contractual privacy commitments in a register mapped to the operational controls that satisfy them, flag where the commitment is stricter than the regulatory baseline, and set the internal deadline inside the contractual one.',
  'Apply the forty-eight hour notice to all customers to keep one process.',
  'C','Contractual privacy obligations are a distinct compliance source that must be inventoried and mapped to controls, or they are discovered only when breached. Applying the strictest clause to everyone is a design choice, not an obligation, and it can be operationally unaffordable.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Programme: Establishing Programme Governance','exam',
  'What is the privacy function''s role under the three lines model?',
  'First line, because it processes personal data.',
  'Second line, setting policy and standards, advising and challenging the business, and monitoring compliance, while the business owns and operates the controls and internal audit provides independent assurance.',
  'Third line, providing independent assurance to the board.',
  'Outside the model entirely.',
  'B','Privacy is an oversight function: it designs the framework and challenges, but the first line owns the risk and the controls. Placing it in the third line would compromise the independence of assurance.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Developing a Privacy Programme','exam',
  'A start-up marketplace has no privacy programme and limited budget. What is the correct first-year sequence?',
  'Buy a consent management platform and a data discovery tool first.',
  'Draft a full policy suite before any assessment.',
  'Establish accountability and scope, build the data inventory and flow map, perform a gap assessment against applicable law and contracts, then prioritise remediation by risk, tooling last where manual controls do not scale.',
  'Certify to an international standard first and derive the programme from the certificate.',
  'C','CIPM sequences accountability, inventory, gap assessment and risk-prioritised remediation, with tooling bought to solve an identified scale problem. Buying tools or certifying before the gap is known spends the budget on the wrong problem.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Assess','exam',
  'The data inventory was completed eighteen months ago and has not been touched. What is the main consequence?',
  'None, inventories are point-in-time documents.',
  'Only the training programme becomes inaccurate.',
  'Every dependent activity degrades: breach scoping, rights fulfilment, retention execution, transfer mapping and vendor oversight all rely on the inventory being current.',
  'The privacy notice becomes legally invalid on its face.',
  'C','The inventory is the substrate of the whole operational lifecycle, so its decay silently breaks the processes built on it. It must be refreshed by change management triggers as well as by periodic review.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Protect','hard',
  'A data processing agreement with an offshore development vendor allows the vendor to use production data for testing. What should the privacy manager require?',
  'Nothing, provided the agreement is signed.',
  'A ban on all offshore development work.',
  'Removal of the permission, with test environments supplied from synthetic or de-identified data, technical enforcement of the restriction, and an access review evidencing that developers cannot reach production personal data.',
  'A confidentiality undertaking from each developer.',
  'C','Using production personal data for testing has no lawful basis in the original collection purpose and multiplies the exposed copies. The fix is technical: de-identified or synthetic test data with enforced access controls, not a promise.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Sustain','exam',
  'Which finding from a privacy audit should be escalated to the audit committee rather than closed with the process owner?',
  'A single expired training record.',
  'A typographical error in the privacy notice.',
  'A missing field description in the inventory.',
  'A systemic failure such as personal data flowing to an unassessed third country with no transfer mechanism, affecting multiple products and requiring investment to remediate.',
  'D','Escalation criteria turn on systemic reach, risk severity and the resources needed to fix, not on the number of findings. Isolated administrative defects belong with the process owner.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Respond','exam',
  'A regulator opens an inquiry into the marketplace''s marketing practices. What should the programme produce?',
  'The programme record: the applicable policies and their version history, the inventory entries and lawful basis analysis for the processing, the notices as served at the relevant time, the consent or objection records, training evidence and the assessments performed.',
  'A statement that the company takes privacy seriously.',
  'Only the current privacy notice.',
  'The names of the individuals responsible.',
  'A','Accountability is demonstrated with contemporaneous documentation showing what was decided, when and on what basis. A programme that cannot produce version history and decision records cannot evidence compliance, however good its practice was.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Programme Framework','exam',
  'How should the programme treat a jurisdiction whose law is less demanding than the global baseline?',
  'Lower the local standard to the legal minimum to reduce cost.',
  'Apply the baseline, note the lower local minimum in the jurisdictional delta register, and lower a specific control only on a documented, risk-accepted decision by the accountable owner.',
  'Withdraw the programme from that jurisdiction.',
  'Apply the strictest law worldwide with no analysis.',
  'B','Consistency is cheaper to operate than divergence, so the baseline holds unless a deliberate, recorded decision lowers it. The delta register is what makes those choices visible and reviewable.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Assess','exam',
  'What distinguishes a privacy threshold assessment from a full data protection impact assessment?',
  'They are the same document at different lengths.',
  'The threshold assessment is performed after launch and the DPIA before.',
  'The threshold assessment is a short screening applied to every change to decide whether the risk indicators require a full DPIA; the DPIA is the deeper analysis of necessity, proportionality, risks and mitigations.',
  'Only the DPIA is documented.',
  'C','Screening everything cheaply and escalating the few that need depth is what makes the assess phase scale. Both are documented, and the threshold assessment is the evidence that the screening decision was made.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Protect','exam',
  'Which control most directly reduces the harm of an insider misusing buyer contact data?',
  'A stronger acceptable use policy.',
  'Annual awareness training.',
  'A confidentiality clause in employment contracts.',
  'Least-privilege access based on role, with periodic access recertification and logging and alerting on bulk export.',
  'D','Technical access limitation and detection reduce the harm an insider can cause; policy and training shape intent but do not constrain capability. Recertification is what stops privilege accumulating as people change roles.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Sustain','exam',
  'What is the value of benchmarking the privacy programme externally?',
  'It replaces the internal maturity assessment.',
  'It guarantees regulatory compliance.',
  'It provides context for the maturity result, helps calibrate investment against peers, and gives the board a reference point when judging whether the programme is proportionate.',
  'It removes the need for audits.',
  'C','Benchmarking supplies calibration and board context, but it is external evidence rather than an assurance activity. It cannot substitute for internal assessment, audit, or a legal gap analysis.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Respond','exam',
  'Under UAE Federal Decree-Law 45/2021, what should the marketplace''s response procedure recognise about the data subject rights it must honour?',
  'The PDPL grants no individual rights.',
  'The PDPL grants only a right of access.',
  'The PDPL grants a right to request information and access, correction, erasure, restriction and objection to processing, and data portability, with a right to complain to the UAE Data Office, so the response procedure must cover each and record the outcome.',
  'The PDPL rights apply only to UAE nationals.',
  'C','Articles 13 to 19 of the PDPL create a rights set broadly parallel to the GDPR, and the operational answer is one intake and fulfilment process serving both. Nationality is not a condition of the rights.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Programme: Establishing Programme Governance','hard',
  'When does the UAE PDPL require the appointment of a data protection officer?',
  'For every controller and processor without exception.',
  'Never; the PDPL has no such role.',
  'Only for entities licensed in the DIFC.',
  'Where processing would cause a high risk to the confidentiality and privacy of the data subject, involves systematic and comprehensive evaluation of sensitive personal data including profiling, or involves large volumes of sensitive personal data; the appointment must be notified to the UAE Data Office.',
  'D','Article 10 of the PDPL sets risk-based triggers rather than a universal duty, similar in shape to GDPR Article 37 but differently worded. The officer may be internal or external and may serve more than one entity.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Developing a Privacy Programme','hard',
  'The marketplace is UAE-mainland licensed but a subsidiary sits in the DIFC. What must the programme account for?',
  'Nothing; the federal PDPL covers both entities uniformly.',
  'That the DIFC entity is exempt from all data protection law.',
  'That the DIFC applies its own Data Protection Law 5/2020 with its own regulator, notification, transfer and breach rules, so the programme must map each entity to its applicable regime and reconcile the differences in one operating model.',
  'That the DIFC entity must apply the GDPR verbatim.',
  'C','The financial free zones operate independent data protection regimes with their own commissioners, and the federal PDPL does not apply inside them. A multi-entity UAE group therefore has more than one applicable law to map.'),
 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Programme Framework','exam',
  'A UAE marketplace serving consumers wants one set of customer-facing commitments. Which non-privacy UAE requirement must the framework also carry?',
  'Only the PDPL.',
  'Only the anti-money-laundering rules.',
  'Only the labour law.',
  'The Consumer Protection Law 15/2020 disclosure and returns obligations and the electronic commerce and electronic transactions requirements on supplier identity, contract terms and record keeping, which sit alongside the PDPL in the customer-facing framework.',
  'D','A customer commitments framework spans privacy, consumer and e-commerce law, and consumer disclosure defects surface in the same channels as privacy complaints. Mapping them together prevents contradictory terms on one page.'),

 ('CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)','Privacy Operational Lifecycle: Protect','exam',
  'A processor contract says the vendor will notify the marketplace of a security incident "as soon as reasonably practicable". Why should the privacy manager reject that wording?',
  'Because the controller''s own regulatory clock starts when it becomes aware, so the notice period must be a defined and short interval, expressed in hours, together with the minimum content of the notice and a named contact, or the controller cannot meet its own deadline.',
  'Because processors are not permitted to notify controllers of incidents at all.',
  'Because the phrase is unenforceable in every jurisdiction.',
  'Because the notice must instead go directly to the regulator from the vendor.',
  'A','A vague notification standard leaves the controller unable to assess and notify inside its own statutory deadline, so the clause must fix an hour count, the content and the contact. The processor notifies the controller, not the authority, so the controller must be able to act on the notice immediately.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Understanding Governance, Risk and Compliance','exam',
  'How do governance, risk management and compliance relate to each other in an integrated GRC model?',
  'Governance sets direction, accountability and the risk appetite; risk management identifies and treats the risks to achieving the objectives set; compliance ensures that the pursuit of those objectives stays within legal, regulatory and internal rules, with all three sharing a common taxonomy and reporting line to the board.',
  'Compliance sits above governance and directs the board.',
  'They are three independent functions that should not share information.',
  'Risk management is a subset of internal audit.',
  'A','ICA teaches GRC as one accountability chain running from board direction through risk treatment to compliance assurance. A shared taxonomy is what allows the three to aggregate into a single board view rather than three unreconciled reports.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Risk Management','exam',
  'A risk register records a residual risk above the stated appetite and the business wants to proceed. What is the correct treatment?',
  'Amend the appetite statement so the risk falls inside it.',
  'Escalate the risk for formal acceptance by the person or committee with authority for that appetite, record the rationale, the compensating controls and a review date, and report the acceptance to the board.',
  'Reduce the recorded impact score to reflect management confidence.',
  'Remove the risk from the register once the business has decided.',
  'B','Exceeding appetite is a decision reserved to the delegated authority, not to the business owner, and it must be documented and time-limited. Adjusting the appetite or the score to fit the decision is the classic failure ICA warns against.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Compliance Frameworks and the Compliance Function','exam',
  'What is the purpose of a compliance monitoring programme as distinct from internal audit?',
  'To test, on a risk-based schedule, whether the controls that mitigate compliance risks are operating as designed, and to feed the findings back to the business and the board as second line assurance.',
  'To replace the internal audit plan.',
  'To provide independent third line assurance to the audit committee.',
  'To investigate staff misconduct.',
  'A','The compliance monitoring programme is second line, risk-based and continuous, sitting between business self-assessment and independent audit. Internal audit retains independence by testing the compliance function as well as the business.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','The Regulatory Environment','hard',
  'A UAE-based marketplace expands into a jurisdiction whose regulator publishes principles rather than detailed rules. What does this change for the compliance function?',
  'Nothing, because principles are not enforceable.',
  'It removes the need for a compliance manual.',
  'It permits a lower standard than a rules-based regime.',
  'Compliance must interpret the outcome the regulator seeks, document the reasoning behind the firm''s chosen approach, and be able to demonstrate that the outcome was achieved, because there is no rule text to point to as evidence of compliance.',
  'D','Principles-based regulation shifts the evidential burden onto the firm''s own reasoning and outcomes. ICA emphasises documenting the interpretation and the outcome achieved, since a tick-list defence is unavailable.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Corporate Governance and Ethics','exam',
  'The founder chairs the board and is also chief executive, with two non-executive directors. What is the governance concern?',
  'None, provided the founder is the majority shareholder.',
  'The concentration of the chair and chief executive roles weakens the board''s ability to challenge management, so the concern is independence of oversight; mitigations include separating the roles, appointing a senior independent director, or reserving specified matters to the non-executives.',
  'The board is too small to be lawful.',
  'The concern is only relevant to listed companies.',
  'B','Separating the chair and chief executive is a core governance principle because the chair''s role is to hold management to account. Where separation is impractical, compensating mechanisms such as a senior independent director and reserved matters are the recognised responses.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Financial Crime Prevention','exam',
  'A new supplier is a company whose ownership chain runs through three jurisdictions and ends in a nominee arrangement. What does risk-based customer due diligence require?',
  'Enhanced due diligence: establish and verify the ultimate beneficial owner behind the nominee, understand the purpose of the structure, obtain senior management approval for the relationship and apply enhanced ongoing monitoring.',
  'Refusal of the relationship in every case.',
  'Standard due diligence, because the supplier is a company rather than an individual.',
  'Reliance on the introducing party''s checks with no further work.',
  'A','Opaque ownership and nominee arrangements are recognised higher-risk indicators that trigger enhanced due diligence rather than refusal. Identifying the ultimate beneficial owner and understanding the rationale for the structure are the substance of that work.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Managing the Relationship with the Regulator','exam',
  'The firm discovers a breach of a regulatory requirement that the regulator has not detected. What is the expected approach?',
  'Self-report promptly, with the facts established so far, the root cause as then understood, the containment and remediation steps and their timetable, updating the regulator as the picture develops.',
  'Wait until remediation is complete so the report is final.',
  'Report only if the regulator opens an inquiry.',
  'Report through an external adviser to preserve privilege over the facts.',
  'A','Regulators expect open and cooperative dealing, and prompt self-reporting with a remediation plan is consistently treated as mitigation. Delaying until everything is known converts a breach into a disclosure failure.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Risk Management','hard',
  'The marketplace has strong controls and reports very few operational risk events. What should the risk function conclude?',
  'That the control environment is proven effective and monitoring can be reduced.',
  'That the risk appetite should be raised.',
  'That risk reporting should move to an annual cycle.',
  'Nothing yet: a low event count may reflect effective controls or an under-reporting culture, so the function should corroborate with independent indicators such as audit findings, complaints, near misses, system logs and staff survey data before concluding.',
  'D','A single quiet number cannot distinguish an effective control from a broken detection or reporting channel. ICA expects corroboration from independent sources before a low event count is read as assurance.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Compliance Frameworks and the Compliance Function','exam',
  'What does an effective compliance risk assessment produce?',
  'A list of all applicable laws.',
  'An inventory of the firm''s compliance obligations mapped to its activities, an assessment of inherent risk, the controls that mitigate each, the residual risk and the resulting risk-based monitoring plan and resource allocation.',
  'The annual training calendar.',
  'The board''s conflicts of interest register.',
  'B','The obligation-to-activity-to-control mapping is what turns a legal inventory into a risk assessment that can drive monitoring and resourcing. Training and conflicts registers are outputs of the framework, not the assessment itself.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Corporate Governance and Ethics','exam',
  'A procurement manager''s brother owns a supplier bidding for a large contract. What does good governance require?',
  'That the interest be declared in the conflicts register, that the manager be recused from the evaluation and award decision, and that the decision be taken and documented by an independent evaluator with the safeguard recorded.',
  'That the supplier be excluded automatically.',
  'That the manager keep the relationship confidential to avoid prejudicing the bid.',
  'That the manager proceed but be supervised informally.',
  'A','A conflict is managed by disclosure, recusal and independent decision-making, with the safeguards documented so the decision withstands later scrutiny. Automatic exclusion of the supplier is neither required nor commercially rational.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Financial Crime Prevention','exam',
  'A staff member notices a buyer paying invoices in structured amounts just below the reporting threshold from unrelated accounts. What is the correct action?',
  'Ask the buyer directly to explain the pattern before doing anything.',
  'Report the suspicion internally to the money laundering reporting officer without delay, and avoid any disclosure to the buyer that could constitute tipping off.',
  'Freeze the account and inform the buyer of the reason.',
  'Record the observation and review it at the next quarterly meeting.',
  'B','Suspicion is escalated internally to the reporting officer, who decides on an external report; the individual does not investigate. Questioning the customer about the suspicion risks the tipping-off offence.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Data Protection and Information Governance','hard',
  'The compliance function wants to monitor staff email for financial crime indicators. What must the governance analysis establish?',
  'A lawful basis and proportionality for the monitoring, transparency to staff through a monitoring policy and notice, limits on scope and retention, restricted access to the results, and consideration of local employment and communications law, which in some jurisdictions restricts monitoring more tightly than data protection law alone.',
  'Only that the monitoring is technically feasible.',
  'Nothing, because the firm owns the email system.',
  'Consent from every employee, which is always a valid basis in the employment context.',
  'A','Employee monitoring is a proportionality problem governed by data protection, employment and communications law together. Consent is generally unreliable in the employment relationship because of the imbalance of power.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Understanding Governance, Risk and Compliance','exam',
  'What does the three lines model allocate to the second line?',
  'Setting the framework, policies and standards, providing expertise and challenge to the first line, and monitoring the adequacy and operation of controls.',
  'Independent assurance reporting to the audit committee.',
  'Ownership and day-to-day operation of the controls.',
  'Approval of the annual accounts.',
  'A','The second line designs and oversees, the first line owns and operates, and the third line independently assures. Confusing the second and first lines is what leaves controls unowned and the oversight function conflicted.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Compliance Frameworks and the Compliance Function','exam',
  'A compliance policy has been in place for three years and staff routinely work around it. What should the compliance function do first?',
  'Reissue the policy with a stronger warning.',
  'Investigate why the workaround exists, since a policy that conflicts with a workable process is a control design failure; then redesign the control so the compliant route is the practical route, and only then enforce.',
  'Discipline the staff who deviate.',
  'Escalate immediately to the regulator.',
  'B','Systematic workarounds are evidence of a design defect rather than of individual misconduct. ICA expects root cause analysis and redesign before enforcement, otherwise the deviation simply becomes less visible.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','The Regulatory Environment','hard',
  'What is the practical significance of extraterritorial regulation for a UAE marketplace?',
  'It is irrelevant unless the firm has an office abroad.',
  'It applies only to sanctions.',
  'It converts foreign law into UAE law automatically.',
  'Obligations under regimes such as the GDPR, foreign sanctions regimes and anti-bribery laws with extraterritorial reach can bind the firm by reason of its customers, its counterparties, its currency of settlement or its group structure, so the compliance universe must be scoped by activity rather than by office location.',
  'D','Scoping the obligation universe by activity and connection, not by physical presence, is the core lesson of extraterritoriality. Sanctions, bribery and data protection are the regimes that most often catch a firm with no foreign office.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Risk Management','exam',
  'What distinguishes a key risk indicator from a key performance indicator?',
  'A key risk indicator is a forward-looking metric selected because its movement signals a change in the level of a specific risk, whereas a key performance indicator measures achievement against an objective.',
  'They are the same measure reported to different audiences.',
  'A key risk indicator is always a financial measure.',
  'A key performance indicator is set by the regulator and a key risk indicator by management.',
  'A','Indicators earn the risk label by their predictive relationship to a defined risk, with a threshold that triggers action. Performance measures may correlate with risk but are not selected for that purpose.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Corporate Governance and Ethics','exam',
  'How should a whistleblowing arrangement be designed to be credible?',
  'An independent and confidential channel with an anonymous option, a route that bypasses line management and reaches the audit committee or a designated non-executive, protection from retaliation stated and enforced, defined triage and investigation steps, and periodic reporting on volumes and outcomes.',
  'Reports are accepted only in writing and only from named employees.',
  'Reports go to the line manager only, to keep matters local.',
  'Reports are reviewed annually by the compliance function.',
  'A','Credibility depends on a route that bypasses the people most likely to be implicated and on visible protection from retaliation. Reporting on volumes and outcomes is what demonstrates the channel is live rather than nominal.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Financial Crime Prevention','exam',
  'Which factor most clearly raises the money laundering risk rating of a marketplace transaction?',
  'The buyer is a company rather than an individual.',
  'The transaction is denominated in a currency other than the dirham.',
  'The order is unusually large for the buyer''s profile.',
  'Payment is made by an unrelated third party in a higher-risk jurisdiction with no commercial explanation.',
  'D','Third-party payments from unconnected accounts, particularly across higher-risk jurisdictions, are a recognised layering indicator. Corporate status, foreign currency and order size alone are ordinary commercial features that require context before they signal risk.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Managing the Relationship with the Regulator','hard',
  'A regulator requests documents that include legally privileged advice on the very issue under review. What is the correct approach?',
  'Hand over everything to demonstrate cooperation.',
  'Refuse the whole request as privileged.',
  'Destroy the advice before responding.',
  'Produce the non-privileged material promptly and in full, identify the privileged material in a schedule with the basis of the claim, and take legal advice on whether to maintain or waive privilege, recognising that waiver may be partial and may not be reversible.',
  'D','Cooperation and privilege are managed together by producing what is not privileged and asserting the claim transparently over what is. Blanket refusal reads as obstruction and blanket disclosure waives protection that cannot be recovered.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Understanding Governance, Risk and Compliance','exam',
  'What is the difference between risk appetite and risk tolerance?',
  'Appetite is the amount and type of risk the organisation is willing to take in pursuit of its objectives, while tolerance is the acceptable variation around that level for a specific risk or objective, expressed as a limit or threshold.',
  'Appetite applies to financial risk and tolerance to operational risk.',
  'Tolerance is set by the regulator and appetite by the board.',
  'They are interchangeable terms.',
  'A','Appetite is the strategic statement and tolerance is its operational expression as measurable limits. Without tolerances the appetite statement cannot be monitored or breached in any testable way.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Compliance Frameworks and the Compliance Function','exam',
  'The marketplace outsources supplier verification to a third party. Where does regulatory responsibility sit?',
  'With the outsourced provider, under the contract.',
  'Jointly, in proportion to the fees paid.',
  'With the firm, which remains fully responsible for compliance and must therefore conduct due diligence on the provider, define the service and controls in the contract, retain the right to audit and access records, monitor performance against defined measures, and hold an exit plan.',
  'With the regulator that authorised the provider.',
  'C','Outsourcing transfers activity but never accountability, which is why the outsourcing controls are due diligence, contract, audit rights, monitoring and exit. A firm cannot answer a regulator by pointing at its vendor.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Data Protection and Information Governance','exam',
  'What does an information governance framework add beyond data protection compliance?',
  'Nothing; the two are the same discipline.',
  'Ownership and classification of all information assets, records management and retention across the full information estate, quality and integrity standards, access governance, and defensible disposal, so that the organisation can trust and evidence its own records regardless of whether they contain personal data.',
  'Only the encryption standards.',
  'Only the rules for board minutes.',
  'B','Information governance covers the whole information estate, including commercial and regulatory records that hold no personal data at all. Data protection is one obligation set that the framework must satisfy among others.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','The Regulatory Environment','hard',
  'A new regulation takes effect in nine months and requires system changes. How should the compliance function run the change?',
  'Wait for the final guidance before starting any work.',
  'Assign the change to the IT department to schedule.',
  'Perform a gap analysis against the current state, quantify the impact by process and system, agree an owned implementation plan with milestones and a board-reported status, engage with the regulator or industry body on interpretation where the text is unclear, and track residual gaps as risks up to the effective date.',
  'Issue a policy update on the effective date.',
  'C','Regulatory change management is a project with a gap analysis, owners, milestones and board visibility, not a policy reissue. Waiting for final guidance routinely leaves too little time for system change.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Risk Management','exam',
  'What does a control self-assessment contribute to the risk framework?',
  'Independent verification of control effectiveness.',
  'A regulatory filing requirement.',
  'A replacement for compliance monitoring.',
  'First line ownership: the business assesses whether its own controls are designed and operating effectively, which surfaces issues early and creates accountability, subject to validation by the second and third lines because self-assessment is not independent.',
  'D','Self-assessment builds first line ownership and early detection, but its lack of independence is exactly why it must be validated. Treating it as assurance in its own right is a common framework failure.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Corporate Governance and Ethics','exam',
  'Which measure most reliably shapes an ethical culture in practice?',
  'A longer and more detailed code of conduct.',
  'Consistent consequence management, where the same standard is visibly applied to senior and junior staff alike, and where incentives and performance measures do not reward outcomes achieved by breaching the code.',
  'An annual ethics survey.',
  'A statement of values on the website.',
  'B','Culture follows what is rewarded and what is punished, not what is written. ICA emphasises the alignment of incentives and the visible consistency of consequences as the primary levers of tone from the top.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Financial Crime Prevention','hard',
  'A sanctions screening system generates a high volume of false positives and the operations team is behind on clearing them. What is the compliance risk?',
  'None, since false positives are by definition not real matches.',
  'The only risk is operational cost.',
  'Alert backlogs delay the detection of true matches and can result in a prohibited payment being processed, so the response is to tune the matching thresholds and data quality with documented testing, resource the queue to clear within a defined service level, and escalate the backlog as a risk while it persists.',
  'The system should be switched off until it is retuned.',
  'C','A backlog converts a detective control into a delayed one, and a genuine match sitting unreviewed is a live breach exposure. Tuning must be evidenced by testing so that reduced alerting is not simply reduced detection.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Managing the Relationship with the Regulator','exam',
  'What is the value of a documented regulatory engagement log?',
  'It satisfies a statutory filing requirement.',
  'It replaces the need for meeting minutes.',
  'It records every material interaction, commitment, deadline and item of guidance received, so that commitments are tracked to completion, positions stay consistent across the organisation, and the firm can evidence its dealings if the relationship is later reviewed.',
  'It is only needed once enforcement action has begun.',
  'C','Commitments made in a meeting are as binding as those made in writing, and inconsistency across the firm damages credibility quickly. The log turns dispersed conversations into tracked obligations.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Compliance Frameworks and the Compliance Function','exam',
  'The board asks for one page on compliance each quarter. What belongs on it?',
  'The full compliance monitoring plan.',
  'The complete regulatory obligation inventory.',
  'A list of all training completed.',
  'The material risks against appetite with owners, the status of monitoring findings and overdue actions, breaches and regulatory interactions, upcoming regulatory change with its impact, and the decisions the board is being asked to take.',
  'D','Board reporting is exception, trend and decision oriented against appetite. Plans, inventories and training logs are working documents that support the report rather than constitute it.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Understanding Governance, Risk and Compliance','exam',
  'Two departments maintain separate risk registers with different scoring scales and no common categories. What is the consequence?',
  'None, provided each register is maintained accurately.',
  'Only a reporting inconvenience.',
  'Risks cannot be aggregated or compared, so the board cannot see concentration or total exposure, and remediation is prioritised locally rather than by enterprise significance; the fix is a common taxonomy, scoring scale and aggregation rule.',
  'The registers must be merged into one department.',
  'C','Enterprise risk management depends on comparability, and divergent scales make aggregation meaningless rather than merely awkward. A common taxonomy and scale is the precondition for a single board view.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Data Protection and Information Governance','hard',
  'The firm plans to keep all customer records indefinitely because storage is cheap and records may be useful later. What is the governance objection?',
  'None, provided the records are secure.',
  'Only the storage cost is objectionable.',
  'Indefinite retention breaches storage limitation and retention obligations, expands the population exposed by any breach, enlarges the burden of every access request and disclosure exercise, and defeats defensible disposal; retention must be set per record class by purpose and by statutory requirement, with legal holds as the documented exception.',
  'The records should be archived offline and kept indefinitely there.',
  'C','Retention is a risk control as well as a legal duty, because data that no longer exists cannot be breached, disclosed or misused. Moving the data offline changes the storage medium, not the obligation or the exposure.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','The Regulatory Environment','exam',
  'How should the compliance function treat regulatory guidance that is not legally binding?',
  'Ignore it, since only binding rules matter.',
  'Treat it as evidence of the regulator''s expectations and of what it regards as good practice, follow it or document a reasoned basis for a different approach that achieves the same outcome, and expect to be asked to justify any divergence.',
  'Adopt it verbatim as internal policy in all cases.',
  'Refer it to the board for a legal opinion before any action.',
  'B','Guidance sets the benchmark against which supervisory judgement is exercised even where it does not bind. Divergence is permissible but must be reasoned, documented and outcome-equivalent.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Risk Management','exam',
  'What is the correct order of the risk management cycle?',
  'Treat, identify, assess, monitor.',
  'Identify, treat, monitor, assess.',
  'Establish the context and objectives, identify risks, analyse and evaluate them against appetite, treat them by avoiding, reducing, transferring or accepting, then monitor, review and report, with communication throughout.',
  'Assess, report, identify, close.',
  'C','Context first is what makes identification complete and evaluation meaningful, and communication runs alongside every step rather than at the end. Treating before assessing spends effort on the wrong risks.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Corporate Governance and Ethics','exam',
  'Why does a governance framework specify matters reserved to the board?',
  'To satisfy the external auditor.',
  'To reduce the number of board meetings.',
  'To transfer liability from management to the board.',
  'To fix, in advance and in writing, the decisions that cannot be delegated, such as strategy, risk appetite, major transactions and the appointment of key function holders, so that authority is unambiguous and delegation elsewhere is bounded and auditable.',
  'D','Reserved matters and the delegated authority matrix together define who may decide what, which is the operational core of governance. Clear boundaries are what make a delegation defensible rather than a gap.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Financial Crime Prevention','hard',
  'A supplier passed onboarding due diligence two years ago and has traded normally since. What does ongoing monitoring require?',
  'Nothing further until the relationship ends.',
  'A full repeat of onboarding due diligence every twelve months for every supplier.',
  'Screening at onboarding only, since the risk is established at that point.',
  'Periodic review on a frequency set by the risk rating, refreshed screening against sanctions and politically exposed person lists, transaction monitoring against the expected profile, and event-driven review when the ownership, jurisdiction, activity or adverse media picture changes.',
  'D','Due diligence is a continuing obligation calibrated to risk, not a gate passed once at onboarding. Trigger events matter as much as the periodic cycle because risk changes between reviews.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Compliance Frameworks and the Compliance Function','exam',
  'What resourcing argument should the compliance officer put to the board when the monitoring plan cannot be completed?',
  'A request for headcount framed by workload volume alone.',
  'A statement that the plan will be completed later in the year.',
  'The specific risks that will go unmonitored, the exposure that creates, the options with their cost, and a recommendation, so that the board either resources the plan or formally accepts the unmonitored risk.',
  'A reduction of the plan to what current resources allow, without escalation.',
  'C','Framing the shortfall as an explicit risk acceptance decision puts the choice where the accountability sits. Quietly trimming the plan to fit resources conceals the exposure and leaves the compliance officer carrying it.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Understanding Governance, Risk and Compliance','exam',
  'What is conduct risk in a marketplace context?',
  'The risk that staff breach the employee handbook.',
  'The risk of a cyber attack on the platform.',
  'The risk that the firm''s behaviour, products, pricing, disclosures or incentives deliver poor outcomes for its customers or damage market integrity, whether or not any specific rule is broken.',
  'The risk of a fine from the tax authority.',
  'C','Conduct risk is outcome-focused and can crystallise without any rule breach, which is what distinguishes it from ordinary compliance risk. Incentive design and disclosure quality are its most common sources.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Data Protection and Information Governance','exam',
  'Under UAE Federal Decree-Law 45/2021, how does the position on lawful processing differ from the GDPR, and what does that mean for the firm''s consent records?',
  'It is identical, so GDPR consent records suffice unchanged.',
  'The PDPL requires no records of any kind.',
  'The PDPL treats the data subject''s consent as the general requirement, with defined exceptions such as contract performance, legal obligation, protection of the public interest and legal claims, so consent capture, its scope and the evidence of withdrawal must be recorded and auditable for UAE processing.',
  'The PDPL permits processing without any basis provided the data stays in the UAE.',
  'C','Article 4 of the PDPL is built as a consent requirement plus enumerated exceptions rather than six equal bases, which raises the evidential importance of consent records for UAE processing. Withdrawal must be as recordable as the original consent.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Financial Crime Prevention','exam',
  'Which activity brings a UAE business within the designated non-financial businesses and professions regime under Cabinet Decision 10/2019?',
  'Operating any online platform that facilitates payment between parties.',
  'Brokering or acting as an agent in the sale and purchase of real estate, dealing in precious metals and precious stones in single or linked cash transactions at or above the prescribed threshold, providing audit or accounting services, or acting as a company or trust service provider.',
  'Holding a mainland trade licence with a commercial activity code.',
  'Employing more than fifty staff in the UAE.',
  'B','The DNFBP categories in Cabinet Decision 10/2019 are activity-based and exhaustive, and platform or payment facilitation alone does not create one. A business that falls within a category must register on goAML and apply the customer due diligence, reporting and record-keeping obligations.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Managing the Relationship with the Regulator','exam',
  'A UAE business determines that it is a DNFBP. What is the immediate registration and reporting obligation?',
  'To notify its bank and no one else.',
  'To register on the goAML system of the Financial Intelligence Unit and on the automatic reporting system for sanctions lists, appoint a compliance officer, and file suspicious transaction reports through goAML when suspicion arises.',
  'To apply to the securities regulator for a licence.',
  'To file an annual return only, with no ongoing reporting.',
  'B','goAML registration and the sanctions notification system are the entry requirements for the UAE AML regime, and reporting flows through goAML rather than to any other body. The appointment of a compliance officer and the customer due diligence programme accompany the registration.'),
 ('ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance','Corporate Governance and Ethics','hard',
  'The marketplace sells to UAE consumers as well as businesses. How should the governance framework treat the Consumer Protection Law 15/2020 obligations relative to its commercial terms?',
  'The commercial terms prevail because the customer accepted them.',
  'Consumer law applies only to physical retail stores.',
  'Consumer protection is a marketing matter rather than a governance one.',
  'The statutory consumer rights on price disclosure, product information, defect remedies and returns operate as a floor that the contract cannot reduce, so the terms, the platform disclosures and the complaint-handling process must be reviewed against that floor and any conflicting clause treated as unenforceable and removed.',
  'D','Consumer protection statutes are mandatory law that overrides contrary contractual terms, so a clause that undercuts them creates enforcement and reputational exposure rather than protection. Governance ownership sits with compliance and legal, not with marketing.')
) as v(cert, area, diff, q, a, b, c, d, k, r)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = v.cert);

do $$
declare
  c text;
  n int;
begin
  foreach c in array array[
    'CIPP/E (Certified Information Privacy Professional/Europe)',
    'CIPM (Certified Information Privacy Manager)',
    'ICA International Diploma in Governance, Risk and Compliance'
  ] loop
    select count(*) into n from public.certification_exam_question where certification = c;
    if n < 40 then
      raise exception 'bank % has only % questions, expected at least 40', c, n;
    end if;
    raise notice 'bank % has % questions', c, n;
  end loop;
end $$;
